A debouncer waits until the user stops typing for a short time, usually 200–500 ms, before it runs the search. Without one, each keystroke fires its own search.

**Why it helps:**

- **Fewer requests.** Typing "javascript" would otherwise send 10 queries. With a debouncer it sends 1. That means less server load, lower API costs and less chance of hitting rate limits.
- **No wasted work.** Results for partial words like "j", "ja" and "jav" are useless because the user has already moved on.
- **Fewer race conditions.** When several requests are in flight, an older one can return last and overwrite newer results. Sending fewer requests makes this less likely. You still need to cancel or ignore stale responses, for example with `AbortController`, to be fully safe.
- **Smoother UI.** It avoids re-rendering results on every keystroke, which would cause flicker and jank.

**Tradeoff:** results show up a little later. Tune the delay so it feels responsive without firing on every keystroke.

```js
function debounce(fn, ms) {
  let t;
  return (...args) => {
    clearTimeout(t);
    t = setTimeout(() => fn(...args), ms);
  };
}

input.addEventListener('input', debounce(e => search(e.target.value), 300));
```

**Debounce vs. throttle:** a debouncer waits until activity stops. A throttle runs at most once every N ms while activity continues. For search-as-you-type you usually want a debouncer.

